  "response": "`crimes context src/petty.ts --format json` returns 4 findings, all severity `low`, but the `agent_risk` ordering is the useful signal — it ranks them differently than severity does:\n\n| # | Finding | Symbol | agent_risk | conf |\n|---|---------|--------|-----------|------|\n| 1 | `return_shape_roulette` | `resolvePaymentResult` L21–32 | **0.44** | 0.73 |\n| 2 | `option_bag_junk_drawer` | `describeCheckout` L10–19 | **0.39** | 0.74 |\n| 3 | `negative_flag_maze` | `canRunBilling` L35 | **0.37** | 0.72 |\n| 4 | `boolean_naming_drift` | L34 (`disableBilling`, `skipRetry`) | 0.27 | 0.80 |\n\n`likely_tests: []` and `test_gap.percentile: 0.5` — nothing in the repo covers this file. That matters for sequencing, see the end.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Return Shape Roulette — `resolvePaymentResult`\n\nEvidence: *3 object-literal return shapes, lowest key overlap 0%, `{ code, message, ok }` vs `{ permanent, url }`.*\n\nThe 0% overlap is the whole problem. Three branches, three disjoint shapes, and the discriminator is different in each one — `ok === false`, \"does `url` exist\", `ok === true`. The redirect branch has **no `ok` field at all**, so a caller writing the obvious `if (!result.ok) handleError(result.code)` treats every redirect as a failure with `code: undefined`.\n\n**What an agent gets wrong today:** ask one to \"add a `pending` result for async captures.\" There's no return type to conform to, so it invents a fourth ad-hoc shape (`{ pending: true, retryAfter }`) matching the file's existing style. Now there are four shapes, still no discriminator, and the `!result.ok` bug has a second instance.\n\n**Refactor** — discriminated union, one required tag on every branch:\n\n```ts\nexport type PaymentResult =\n  | { kind: \"error\"; code?: string; message?: string }\n  | { kind: \"redirect\"; url?: string; isPermanent: boolean }\n  | { kind: \"success\"; value?: string; isCached: boolean };\n\nexport function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {\n  if (input.error) return { kind: \"error\", code: input.code, message: input.message };\n  if (input.redirect) return { kind: \"redirect\", url: input.url, isPermanent: false };\n  return { kind: \"success\", value: input.value, isCached: false };\n}\n```\n\nThe explicit annotation is what closes the agent hole: a fourth branch now fails to compile until it's added to `PaymentResult`, and `switch (r.kind)` gives exhaustiveness checking at every call site. This is the only one of the three that changes the runtime payload — callers reading `.ok` must migrate.\n\n## 2. Option Bag Junk Drawer — `describeCheckout`\n\nEvidence: *parameter `options`, 6 distinct property reads: currency, plan, region, retry, role, status.*\n\n`CheckoutOptions` is typed, so the detector isn't complaining about missing types — it's complaining that six unrelated fields arrive through one door. Two concerns are fused: stable identity (`plan`, `role`, `region`, `currency`) and per-attempt state (`status`, `retry`). And the function's real contract is invisible: it emits a **positional** colon-joined key, so array order is load-bearing while nothing in the type says so.\n\n**What an agent gets wrong today:** ask one to \"include the coupon in the checkout description.\" It adds `coupon: string` to the interface and appends `options.coupon` to the join — a one-line, type-clean edit that silently changes the key format for every existing consumer (cache keys, log greps, dedupe sets). Reordering or alphabetizing the array is likewise a no-op to the compiler and a break to everything downstream.\n\n**Refactor** — split the bag by lifetime, and make the serialization order an explicit, named artifact:\n\n```ts\nexport interface CheckoutIdentity { plan: string; role: string; region: string; currency: string }\nexport interface CheckoutAttempt { status: string; retry: boolean }\n\n/** Order is part of the wire contract — appending changes every existing key. */\nconst CHECKOUT_KEY_ORDER = [\"plan\", \"role\", \"region\", \"currency\", \"status\", \"retry\"] as const;\n\nexport function describeCheckout(identity: CheckoutIdentity, attempt: CheckoutAttempt): string {\n  const parts = { ...identity, retry: String(attempt.retry), status: attempt.status };\n  return CHECKOUT_KEY_ORDER.map((k) => parts[k]).join(\":\");\n}\n```\n\nTwo narrow records mean a caller that only has identity can't be forced to fabricate a `retry`, and the named constant turns \"append a field\" from an invisible edit into a deliberate one.\n\n## 3. Negative Flag Maze — `canRunBilling`\n\nEvidence: *negative flags `disableBilling`, `skipRetry`; condition `!disableBilling && !skipRetry`.* Finding #4 (`boolean_naming_drift`) fires on the same line — same root cause, same fix.\n\n**What an agent gets wrong today**, two ways:\n\n- **The De Morgan flip.** Asked to relax the gate — \"either flag alone shouldn't block billing\" — the natural edit is `!disableBilling || !skipRetry`. That reads as \"not both,\" which is the opposite of what was asked, and no test catches it.\n- **The positional swap.** Both params are bare `boolean`. `canRunBilling(skipRetry, disableBilling)` at a call site type-checks perfectly while being exactly backwards. This is the more likely failure of the two, because it happens at the *call* site, outside the context window of whoever is reading this file.\n\nThere's also a scope smell worth flagging: a function named `canRunBilling` gates on retry policy, and `if (cond) return true; return false;` is a boolean identity.\n\n**Refactor** — invert to positive names, take a named object, return the predicate directly:\n\n```ts\nexport interface BillingGate {\n  isBillingEnabled: boolean;\n  isRetryEnabled: boolean;\n}\n\nexport function canRunBilling({ isBillingEnabled, isRetryEnabled }: BillingGate): boolean {\n  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryEnabled;\n}\n```\n\nThe object param kills the swap bug outright — the compiler now requires the names. If the negative flags come from config keys you don't own (`DISABLE_BILLING`), invert once at the config boundary rather than threading `disable*` inward.\n\n---\n\n**Sequencing.** `likely_tests` is empty — none of this is protected. Refactors 1 and 3 change behaviour visible to callers (the `ok`→`kind` migration, and the signature change from positional to named). Land three characterization tests against the *current* behaviour first, then do #3 and #2 (mechanical, compiler-enforced) before #1, which needs a call-site sweep.\n\n*Footnote: `evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app` is a symlink to `examples/messy-ts-app`, so the `context` run resolved into the real repo's git history and picked up `churn: 0.05`. That's why its `agent_risk` values sit ~0.01 above the scan output in the prompt — the ranking is unchanged.*",
